Orthogonal Unit Vectors in Right-Handed.content:
In a strict right-handed Cartesian coordinate system, the vector cross products of the fundamental orthogonal unit vectors (\vec{i}, \vec{j}, \vec{k}) are governed by a set of rigid cyclic relationships:

 \vec{i} \times \vec{j} = \vec{k} 
 \vec{j} \times \vec{k} = \vec{i} 
 \vec{k} \times \vec{i} = \vec{j} 

These equations define the forward, positive cyclic permutations. The order of multiplication is strictly maintained. Moving alphabetically through the cycle (X to Y, Y to Z, Z to X) always generates the positive version of the remaining unit vector. 

Because the cross product is violently non-commutative, reversing any of these pairs mathematically flips the resulting vector by 180 degrees, introducing a negative sign:

 \vec{j} \times \vec{i} = -\vec{k} 
 \vec{k} \times \vec{j} = -\vec{i} 
 \vec{i} \times \vec{k} = -\vec{j} 

Finally, we must account for the self-interaction of unit vectors. The cross product fundamentally relies on the sine of the angle between the vectors. The angle between any unit vector and itself is exactly zero degrees. Because \sin(0^\circ) = 0, the cross product of any unit vector with itself yields the null vector:

 \vec{i} \times \vec{i} = \vec{0} 
 \vec{j} \times \vec{j} = \vec{0} 
 \vec{k} \times \vec{k} = \vec{0} 

Note that the result is technically the zero vector (\vec{0}), which has a magnitude of zero and undefined direction, not the scalar number 0, though they are often treated similarly in basic algebra. These nine fundamental identities are the absolute bedrock required to expand and calculate the cross product of any two arbitrary vectors expressed in Cartesian component form.

